## Why This Is Asked
Assesss ability to architect latency-aware, resilient notify pipelines with per-region strategies, idempotence, and controlled degradation.

## Key Concepts
- Per-region traffic shaping, backpressure, local buffering
- Idempotent delivery and deduplication
- Fast-path caching and back-off strategies
- Canary deployments, runbooks, and rollback criteria

## Code Example
```javascript
// Simple idempotent delivery using Redis
const redis = require('redis');
const client = redis.createClient();

async function deliver(msgId, payload) {
  const key = `notif:${msgId}`;
  return new Promise((resolve, reject) => {
    client.setnx(key, 1, (err, res) => {
      if (err) return reject(err);
      if (res === 1) {
        client.expire(key, 3600, () => resolve(smsGateway.send(payload)));
      } else {
        resolve("duplicate");
      }
    });
  });
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you measure and bound tail latency across regions during gateway outages?
- What alerting would you configure to trigger runbooks and rollback?

## Why This Is Asked

This question probes a beginner-friendly angle on data integrity under deploy pressure, focusing on per-tenant observability, controlled backpressure, and data replay safety.

## Key Concepts

- Per-tenant observability: lag, offsets, retry counts
- Ingress throttling and traffic-splitting
- Idempotent processing and dedup keys
- Rollback and data validation windows

## Code Example

```javascript
// Simple deduplicated idempotent processor
const seen = new Set();
function handle(event){
  const id = event.tenant + ":" + event.id;
  if (seen.has(id)) return;
  seen.add(id);
  // process event
}
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How would you test the rollback plan in a staging env?
- What telemetry would you add to verify data integrity after rollback?

## Why This Is Asked

Tests practical cache resilience, backpressure handling, and measurable rollback criteria under high-traffic scenarios.

## Key Concepts

- Cache-aside with stale-while-revalidate
- Per-key TTLs and background refresh
- Idempotent operations and bounded retries
- Circuit breakers and traffic shaping
- Canary validation and rollback triggers

## Code Example

```javascript
// Pseudo code: safe cache fetch with S-W-R
async function get(key){
  let v = await cache.get(key);
  if (v !== null) return v;
  try {
    let fresh = await origin.fetch(key);
    await cache.set(key, fresh, ttl);
    return fresh;
  } catch (e) {
    // serve stale if available
    let stale = await cache.get(key);
    if (stale != null) return stale;
    throw e;
  }
}
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How would you instrument early stampede detection signals and alerting thresholds?
- What strategies ensure consistent rollout across regional CDNs during a rollback?

## Why This Is Asked
Tests ability to craft an actionable, scalable incident response for a complex streaming pipeline with multi-tenant QoS, data correctness guarantees, and real-time rollback requirements.

## Key Concepts
- Observability: per-tenant backlog metrics, distributed traces, structured logs
- Backpressure handling: tenant-aware throttling, dynamic rebalancing
- Data correctness: idempotency, exactly-once semantics, DLQ handling
- Rollback safety: canary, traffic shaping, validated resumption

## Code Example
```javascript
// Pseudo-idempotent write with dedupe key
async function process(msg){
  const id = msg.id;
  if(await dedupe.has(id)) return;
  await warehouse.write(msg.payload);
  await dedupe.set(id, true, TTL);
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you test the roll-forward vs rollback paths?
- Which metrics define success for resuming traffic, and how are tenants verified?

## Why This Is Asked

This question probes practical incident engineering: how to fuse disparate signals, assign accountability, and make safe change decisions under pressure.

## Key Concepts

- Multi-signal correlation across metrics/logs/traces
- SLO-aligned alert routing and ownership mapping
- Canary and synthetic testing for regression prevention
- RCA templating and rollback playbooks

## Code Example

```javascript
// Simple rule mapping alerts to on-call owners
const owners = {
  payments: "team-payments",
  auth: "team-auth",
  inventory: "team-inventory"
};
function route(alert) {
  const svc = alert.tags?.service || "general";
  return owners[svc] || "oncall";
}
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How would you test the end-to-end runbook in a staging environment?
- What metrics would you collect to validate the success of the rollback strategy?
